+## 2026-11-17 BUG-046: the merge endpoint named by what it holds, not by how it is spelled, at round 3 (R-082)
+
+- **Question:** how the permission guard should recognise a `gh api` call on a pull request's merge endpoint, now that the method is read for nothing. Round 1's brief ruled a normalised pattern (lower-cased, `%2f` and `%2e` decoded, dot segments resolved, `/pulls/<segment>/merge`); both reviewers found escaped letters passing and the supervisor found dot segments in a query erasing the path. Round 2's brief decoded every escape and cut at the first `?` or `#`; both reviewers found escaped delimiters and an escaped slash hiding the path. Each time the brief's own rule was the defect.
+- **Decision: at round 3, the ceiling, under its class line `safety mechanism`, the design changed: a `gh api` word whose decoded, lower-cased text holds both `pulls` and `merge` is the merge endpoint**, refused whatever its method; one decoding per word serves this rule and the merge-mutation rule. Ordinary words holding both names are refused with it, an accepted over-refusal. The placeholder route both round-3 reviewers named (`GH_REPO` or the current branch filling `{repo}`, `:repo`, `{branch}`) is outside the guard's threat-model row and recorded as a lead (docs/HARDENING.md, BUG-046).
+- **Reasoning.** A spelling rule must agree with gh's and GitHub's reading of escapes, delimiters and dot segments; two rounds showed each next reviewer finds a spelling read apart, a defeated safety mechanism that sends the work back every time. GitHub routes the merge only on a path whose segments are `pulls` and `merge`, and gh sends its one endpoint word with its escapes kept, so after decoding every such word holds both names: a rule on what the word contains needs no model of the spelling. The cost is refusing a few ordinary calls the bridge never makes. This is TASK-083's lesson (name what is accepted) applied at the round it was due.
+
 ## 2026-11-17 TASK-083: the guard reads a merge command as gh reads it, over four rounds; `gh api`'s walk drawn to BUG-046 (R-083)
 
 - **Question:** how far TASK-083 must go in making the permission guard read `gh pr merge` as gh itself reads it, now that the merge is held on the ready record `<pr>-<head>` and the number and sha come from the command's words; and where the line is drawn for the guard's older code the new rule rests on. Round 1 built `ready`, `prove_ci`, `premerge-records`' proof and the guard's `ready_recorded`; Review A rejected it on a cluster of short flags (`gh pr merge -sb 12 34`, 34 merged on 12's record) and the audit on two readings older than TASK-083 (a flag's value read as `--match-head-commit`; `--disable-auto` as a flag's value exempting the command). Round 2 read the words after `merge` as gh's flag parser does; both reviewers rejected it on a true/false exemption set again by its `=` form and on `gh_words`, older still, reading an unknown option before the subcommand as one word. Round 3 cleared an exemption on any `=` form and made any option before the subcommand unreadable; both rejected it on an empty word (`gh pr '' merge`) that cobra passes over. Round 4 admits only a command's name before the subcommand.
