+### TASK-083's gate, after its records (2026-11-17, PR #176)
+- **Review B's round 1** (gpt-5.6-sol-high, launch 03:58:20Z) approved TASK-083, confirming round 4's class line; `verify TASK-083-r1` OK, `pr-activity-check.py 176` OK, `premerge TASK-083-r1 176` OK, CI green. **`review-guard.py ready 176`, the first live use of TASK-083's own command** (the branch's copy, the installed release being older), answered READY for head 765f9b4a1a27 on the base branch's required contexts (main); merged as f3fa7b4 with `--match-head-commit`. No records pull request was needed; this entry carries the gate's end.
+
+### BUG-046: the guard's `gh api` merge refusal read as gh reads it (2026-11-17, three builder rounds)
+- **The defect.** Found by TASK-083's round-4 security audit (ruling 2 above): in `_check_word_list`'s `gh api` walk the method loop read a value-shaped word as the method (`gh api -X PUT repos/o/r/pulls/5/merge -p -XGET` passed), and the file-body refusal held only where the endpoint word was exactly `graphql` (`gh api https://api.github.com/graphql -F query=@q.graphql` passed). Reproduced in process (docs/debug/BUG-046.md). Before the brief, gh 2.97.0 was checked locally by `--help` only: it matches command names neither by case nor by prefix (`gh PR MERGE` is an unknown command), so Review A's question from TASK-083 closed with no fix. The supervisor's tests, tests/test_bug046.py, red on f3fa7b4: 32 failed, 18 passed.
+- **Round 1.** F1 a body the guard cannot read (`--input` in any form, a word beginning `@` or holding `=@`) refused whatever the endpoint; F2 the merge endpoint refused whatever the method, the word lower-cased, `%2f`/`%2e` decoded and resolved by `posixpath.normpath`, the method loop deleted; F3 `tests/test_permission_guard.py`'s allowed read of the merge endpoint moved to the refused list. The supervisor's probe found a regression: normpath over the whole word let a `..` in a query or fragment, or a trailing `merge/..`, erase the merge path (refused on f3fa7b4). Review A rejected and the security audit failed it on the same blocker, the brief's error: only `%2f` and `%2e` were decoded, so an escaped letter (`%70ulls`, `merg%65`) passed.
+- **Round 2.** G1 every `%XX` decoded until stable; G2 the endpoint looked for in the decoded word, in its path before the first `?` or `#`, and in that path resolved; G3 the merge-mutation names looked for in the decoded word too; G5 the dead `WRITE_METHODS` deleted. Supervisor's cases red on round 1's tree: 28 failed, 84 passed. Review A rejected and the audit failed it on the same blocker, the brief's error again: it decoded before cutting, where gh cuts only at a literal `?` or `#`, so an escaped `%3F` or `%23` at any nesting stayed inside the path gh sends and hid the `..` after it (`repos/o/r/pulls/5/%23/../merge`), and a decoded `%2F` split one segment in two.
+- **Round 3 (at the ceiling, its class line `safety mechanism`, confirmed by Review A against round 2's code).** The family met twice, the design changed rather than a third spelling closed: K1 a `gh api` word whose decoded, lower-cased text holds both `pulls` and `merge` is the merge endpoint (no cut, no resolution, no pattern); K2 one decoding per word for both refusals; K3 `import posixpath` deleted. The premise: a request GitHub routes to `/repos/<o>/<r>/pulls/<n>/merge` carries both names in gh's one endpoint word, whatever escapes or dot segments surround them. Supervisor's cases red on round 2's tree: 18 failed, 120 passed. Review A approved; the security audit passed it. The supervisor's own run of the Done files: `1986 passed, 20 skipped`. The line is drawn here (docs/CHANGES.md, 2026-11-17).
+- **BUG-046's mutation run (2026-11-17, on round 3's tree).** 12 single-line mutants, each listing every test it fails in tests/test_bug046.py: the body refusal off, or for GraphQL only; the endpoint refusal off; the endpoint read undecoded; decoded once; the mutation names read undecoded; the endpoint not lower-cased; `merge` alone or `pulls` alone; `=@`, `--input=` and the `--input` word each not read. counts: run=12 cached=0 killed=12 survived=0 not applied=0 error=0 expected survive=0 (run/agent/BUG-046-r3-mutation.out). The new tests against the checkpoint 3d6e4eb (f3fa7b4's guard with the supervisor's first cases; `--checkpoint 3d6e4eb`, exit 4): of 144 test ids, 96 fail on the old code and 48 pass there: 32 ordinary calls the fix keeps passing, and 16 refusals f3fa7b4 already made through its PUT-only pattern (the full URL and the seven round-1 spellings round 1 had regressed) (run/agent/BUG-046-r3-checkpoint.out).
+- **Rulings.** (1) The over-refusal K1 brings is accepted: an ordinary `gh api` word holding both names (`'repos/o/r/pulls?q=merged'`, a field `body=merge the pulls`, `repos/o/r/pulls/5/mergeable-report`) is refused; the bridge makes no such call (Review A searched `bridge/`). (2) Both reviewers of round 3 named the same route, outside the guard's threat-model row (docs/INVENTORY.md, Decisions: a value a program takes from the environment): gh fills `{owner}`, `{repo}`, `:owner`, `:repo` and `{branch}` in the endpoint and in `-F` values from `GH_REPO` or the current branch, so the two names can be split between the environment and the word (`GH_REPO=o/pulls gh api -X PUT repos/o/r/:repo/5/merge`; a branch named `pulls/5/merge` and `repos/o/r/{branch}`; a mutation name assembled in a `-F` value). Recorded, not blocking: not a regression (rounds 1 and 2 needed a literal `pulls` too), and a deliberate split no agent types by habit; the habitual `repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/5/merge` holds both names and is refused. Gh's placeholder behaviour is the reviewers' reading, not run (no network).
+- **The builder's assumptions, dispositioned.** Round 1: `posixpath.normpath` resolves `..` as GitHub would, and a `..` in a query is collapsed with the path (the second was the supervisor's finding, closed by round 2; both moot under round 3, which resolves nothing). Round 2: no Semgrep run (accepted: CI's security floor runs it); a non-ASCII escape decoded to the character its hex digits name (accepted: the reading refuses more). Round 3: no Semgrep run (accepted, the same).
+- **Leads, recorded, not fixed.** (1) The placeholders of ruling 2; a fix would read a leading `GH_REPO=` assignment with gh's words, or refuse a placeholder while one is assigned. (2) The decoding is quadratic on nested `%25` with no length check inside its loop; the hook's 15-second limit governs and both round-2 reviewers found it unreachable in ordinary use (a Windows command line of 32 KB decodes in 0.27 s; Linux's 128 KiB in about 4 s). (3) F1's over-refusals: `--jq '@tsv'` and `-f body=@name`. (4) PowerShell refuses `-f body='x'` (the quote not at the word's start), older than BUG-046. (5) Not taken, outside the merge refusal, GitHub's branch rules deciding a direct write to a protected branch: REST `POST repos/<o>/<r>/merges` and `PATCH repos/<o>/<r>/git/refs/heads/<b>`, the GraphQL `mergeBranch`, `updateRef`, `updateRefs` and `createCommitOnBranch`; `gh.cmd`, `gh.com` and a gh named by path as the command's head.
+- **Lesson (TASK-083's, met again).** Rounds 1 and 2 each named a spelling rule for the endpoint and each next review found a spelling gh sends that the rule read apart; the briefs' own rules were the defect both times. Round 3 named what the endpoint must contain rather than how it may be spelled. A rule that must agree with another program's reading names what it accepts at the round the family is first met, not the round after.
